<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upemba Depression</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Upemba Depression is a low-lying basin of lakes, marshes and fertile land along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba River</a> in the south-east of the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Archaeological sites there show long continuous occupation. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba Empire</a> identify it as the heartland in which the empire emerged, its soils sustaining dense populations and its trade linking it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upemba-depression.jpg" alt="Upemba Depression" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/Crew of STS-57 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Upemba_STS057-104-62.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Luba Empire emerged in the <b>Upemba Depression</b> of present-day south-eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, where archaeological evidence indicates continuous occupation from at least the 5th century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
